1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,360
It was the dividing line for a city, a continent, and a way of life.

2
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:09,200
It was the Iron Curtain made of concrete.

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:14,960
A 96-mile barrier that imprisoned over a million people for 28 years.

4
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:21,580
At least 239 people died trying to get over, under, around, and through it.

5
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:25,700
Now, the Berlin Wall on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,820
A scant decade and a half after its fall,

7
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:44,540
there's not much left of the notorious Berlin Wall.

8
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:50,600
The barrier that once split the world is now a relic,

9
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:53,460
innocuous, decomposing,

10
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:56,860
overrun with bushes and weeds,

11
00:00:56,861 --> 00:01:03,220
Guard towers that represented a bullet in the back to anyone trying to climb over the wall

12
00:01:03,221 --> 00:01:07,640
today stand quietly out of place in apartment courtyards

13
00:01:07,641 --> 00:01:11,360
and neighborhood parks.

14
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:19,980
The longest surviving stretch of the wall, a 4,200-foot run known as the East Side Gallery,

15
00:01:19,981 --> 00:01:22,820
is more noted today for its art than its past.

16
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:34,340
But in its time, the Berlin Wall stood as the most menacing, implacable, spookily evocative piece of infrastructure in the world.

17
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:40,700
This was the central fault line of the Cold War.

18
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:43,660
The dividing line between East and West.

19
00:01:44,380 --> 00:01:45,580
Communism and capitalism.

20
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:49,540
The two dominant and irreconcilable systems of our time.

21
00:01:50,740 --> 00:01:52,880
These were tectonic plates.

22
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:55,320
They were moving towards each other.

23
00:01:55,321 --> 00:02:00,720
that they were rubbing against each other in a way which is very difficult to comprehend.

24
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:03,780
It was the matter of moving from one country to the next.

25
00:02:03,880 --> 00:02:06,480
It was a matter of moving from one street corner to the next,

26
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:09,220
and you move from one ideology to the next.

27
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:12,580
The world ends here for capitalism.

28
00:02:12,581 --> 00:02:15,840
A better socialist society starts here.

29
00:02:16,620 --> 00:02:17,920
I was convinced of it.

30
00:02:17,921 --> 00:02:18,621
Oh, he does.

31
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:24,120
Ideology aside, the Berlin Wall was also a monumental,

32
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:26,700
if bizarre, feat of construction and design.

33
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:32,200
A 96-mile double barrier, complete with hundreds of watchtowers

34
00:02:32,201 --> 00:02:35,780
and a horrific death strip that aimed at nothing less

35
00:02:35,781 --> 00:02:38,600
than the absolute division of a major European capital.

36
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:45,720
It was a poor country trying to construct the pyramids

37
00:02:45,721 --> 00:02:47,580
in the middle of a city.

38
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:53,200
And they did it slightly on the cheek because they had to.

39
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:55,040
They didn't have any alternative.

40
00:02:55,560 --> 00:03:01,340
But even so, they did a hell of a job in making it very, very difficult to get out of.

41
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:03,140
Very difficult indeed.

42
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:10,620
The roots and the desperate logic of the war go back to the end of World War II

43
00:03:10,621 --> 00:03:13,900
and the unraveling of relations between former allies.

44
00:03:13,901 --> 00:03:23,620
At war's end, Germany was divided into four zones, one each for the United States, Britain, France, and the Soviet Union.

45
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:33,820
The capital city of Berlin lay 110 miles inside the Soviet zone, and it too was divided in four, one sector for each of the Allies.

46
00:03:33,821 --> 00:03:45,569
The Soviets, who took Berlin after weeks of fierce fighting, laid claim to the largest sector, East Berlin, which occupied about 40% of the city and contained 1.1

47
00:03:45,570 --> 00:03:47,020
million inhabitants.

48
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:53,020
The U.S., Britain and France had control over West Berlin and its 2.2 million citizens.

49
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:59,460
The 200-year-old Brandenburg Gate was one of the few structures to survive the war.

50
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:03,140
It stood at the dividing line between the Soviet and Allied sectors.

51
00:04:03,820 --> 00:04:05,420
between East and West Berlin.

52
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:11,480
Post-war Berlin was a wasteland,

53
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:14,100
and refugees poured from the East into the West,

54
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:16,920
where reconstruction, fueled by the Marshall Plan,

55
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:18,340
was dramatic.

56
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:21,620
To staunch the flow,

57
00:04:21,920 --> 00:04:25,780
the Soviets imposed a land blockade around West Berlin in 1948,

58
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:28,180
designed to starve the Allies out

59
00:04:28,181 --> 00:04:31,040
and bring the entire city under Soviet control.

60
00:04:31,041 --> 00:04:36,240
It was foiled in dramatic fashion by an American airlift.

61
00:04:36,780 --> 00:04:40,660
Nonstop sorties, one every two minutes, 24 hours a day,

62
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:43,700
supplied all the needs of the city for 11 months.

63
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:49,140
The Soviets finally lifted the blockade on May 12, 1949.

64
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:54,360
By now, the former Allies were bitterly at odds.

65
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:56,480
And yet, despite political differences,

66
00:04:56,481 --> 00:05:00,720
the border between East and West Berlin was surprisingly open and free.

67
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:04,080
The 800-year-old city shared a common infrastructure.

68
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:08,440
Everything from mail delivery to subways was jointly operated,

69
00:05:08,860 --> 00:05:12,120
and some 60,000 East Berliners worked on the west side.

70
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:18,120
That was the real problem in Berlin, that it was an open city,

71
00:05:18,121 --> 00:05:21,420
and people could be spot-checked by the police,

72
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:26,060
but they couldn't really get a handle on people crossing between the two halves of the city.

73
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:29,940
Over the next decade, the refugee problem worsened.

74
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:33,540
East Germany was losing over 100,000 people a year,

75
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:36,120
three-quarters of them through Berlin,

76
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:39,000
and Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev

77
00:05:39,001 --> 00:05:42,360
was renewing demands for Allied troops to leave the West.

78
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:46,500
The regime finds out in 1958

79
00:05:46,501 --> 00:05:49,200
that they're actually losing more doctors in a year

80
00:05:49,201 --> 00:05:51,100
than they can actually train in a year,

81
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:52,860
so they're beginning to go backwards.

82
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:57,620
In 1961, when East Germany was starting literally to melt away to the West

83
00:05:57,621 --> 00:05:58,900
because the West was prosperous,

84
00:05:58,901 --> 00:06:03,220
The East German government examined the options, and there was only one option,

85
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:05,160
and that was to build a wall to stop them going.

86
00:06:05,660 --> 00:06:06,840
There was nothing else they could do.

87
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:15,840
And yet, what happened at midnight, August 13th, 1961, caught everyone by surprise.

88
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:23,700
In the history of the planet, no one had ever thought of trying to forcibly divide a city the size of Philadelphia in two with a wall.

89
00:06:23,701 --> 00:06:28,380
There was a huge CIA presence in West Berlin

90
00:06:28,381 --> 00:06:31,900
looking for what the East German regime would have to do

91
00:06:31,901 --> 00:06:33,460
to staunch the flow of the refugees.

92
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:38,460
They concluded that bisecting the city was impossible.

93
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:39,840
This was not an option.

94
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:46,760
To take an old-fashioned, intertwined, unified European capital city

95
00:06:46,761 --> 00:06:48,860
that's been there for 1,000 years

96
00:06:48,861 --> 00:06:52,140
and split it down all boundary lines, can't be done.

97
00:06:52,141 --> 00:07:03,905
Code named Wall of China by the East Germans, the top secret operation aimed to establish a 27-mile barrier between East and West Berlin and a 69-mile barrier between

98
00:07:03,906 --> 00:07:05,960
West Berlin and East Germany.

99
00:07:07,060 --> 00:07:14,720
Foreigners would still be allowed across the border through a handful of checkpoints, but East Germans, especially East Berliners, would not.

100
00:07:14,721 --> 00:07:21,660
The operation, conceived by East German President Walter Ulbricht and approved by Khrushchev,

101
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:25,840
was launched at midnight Sunday morning to catch the city off guard.

102
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:34,140
Within 24 hours, barbed wire had been nailed onto concrete posts through 5.16 miles of

103
00:07:34,141 --> 00:07:34,841
central Berlin.

104
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:40,400
67 of the 81 crossing points between the east and west were sealed off.

105
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:43,620
Movement through the remaining checkpoints was strictly controlled.

106
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:54,060
I listened to the news at 7 a.m., August 13th, and I immediately woke my brother.

107
00:07:54,740 --> 00:08:03,680
We then took our bicycle and drove along the borders and saw how the concrete holes were inserted into the asphalt with the help of jackhammers.

108
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:11,280
Barbed wire barriers were built and a lot of military men carrying weapons, and it looked like war.

109
00:08:11,281 --> 00:08:12,520
It's like war.

110
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:43,640
If the people had rioted, they were in deep trouble.

111
00:08:43,940 --> 00:08:45,820
If the soldiers had said, we're not manning this,

112
00:08:45,860 --> 00:08:46,840
they would have been in deep trouble.

113
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:50,140
It was a desperate throw, such a thing had never been tried.

114
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:52,380
There were no rehearsals, nothing.

115
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:55,840
When it was clear that there was not going to be an uprising,

116
00:08:56,340 --> 00:08:58,560
East German and Soviet officials held their breath

117
00:08:58,561 --> 00:09:00,060
over an even bigger question.

118
00:09:00,580 --> 00:09:03,960
How would the West, in particular the United States, react?

119
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:06,220
West Berlin was in an uproar,

120
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:08,680
with mass demonstrations and some rioting.

121
00:09:08,681 --> 00:09:12,280
But it was the U.S. that Ulbricht and Khrushchev were worried about.

122
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:18,120
The East German authorities are terrified that the West might actually respond,

123
00:09:18,600 --> 00:09:19,460
that there might be a war,

124
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:23,760
so that they're being very careful not to provoke the West.

125
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:27,420
And there are orders that if they shoot,

126
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:31,400
they shouldn't shoot so that any bullets enter West Berlin.

127
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:35,800
They can only shoot parallel to the wall or back into territory.

128
00:09:35,801 --> 00:09:41,040
In Washington, President Kennedy closely monitored events.

129
00:09:41,041 --> 00:09:46,380
But as the days went by, it became clear that as long as the East left West Berlin alone,

130
00:09:46,381 --> 00:09:48,380
he was not going to take military action.

131
00:09:48,381 --> 00:09:56,420
The erection of the wall was politically accepted by the Western allies, because this

132
00:09:56,421 --> 00:10:02,300
reduced or even eliminated a source for future conflict.

133
00:10:02,301 --> 00:10:05,180
the frontier was set.

134
00:10:06,520 --> 00:10:11,780
And no more people moving from East Germany to West Germany,

135
00:10:11,900 --> 00:10:12,860
East Berlin to West Berlin,

136
00:10:12,861 --> 00:10:20,920
and creating some kind of something which you could not calculate.

137
00:10:21,480 --> 00:10:26,480
So in some senses, the building of the wall solved the Berlin crisis,

138
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:30,660
and all sides heaved a huge sigh of relief about that.

139
00:10:30,661 --> 00:10:36,080
They wouldn't say that in public, but that's what a lot of Western politicians thought at the time.

140
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:42,160
After three days, Khrushchev and Ulbricht concluded that the US would not strike back.

141
00:10:42,780 --> 00:10:46,100
On August 16th, they issued orders to put up permanent barriers.

142
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:52,280
Soldiers and workers began hammering together what would become known as the First Generation Wall,

143
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,960
a hodgepodge built up out of whatever materials lay at hand.

144
00:10:56,960 --> 00:11:00,120
Stone and brick were used to divide East and West Berlin.

145
00:11:00,660 --> 00:11:06,100
Barbed wire and watchtowers would lock off the rural border between West Berlin and East Germany.

146
00:11:07,220 --> 00:11:13,400
Western analysts puzzled over how the East Germans managed to hide the resources needed to erect a 100-mile wall.

147
00:11:14,860 --> 00:11:17,760
But the answer was staring everyone in the face.

148
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:22,600
East Berlin was a building site because we bombed it flat.

149
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:29,620
All they had to do was go along to the building sites and commandeer whatever they wanted in terms of concrete slabs,

150
00:11:29,621 --> 00:11:33,688
breeze blocks, bricks, anything they could lay hands on.

151
00:11:33,689 --> 00:11:35,940
A very crude, simple structure.

152
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:45,860
So crude that it was laid without a foundation, the wall's own weight would have to suffice to

153
00:11:45,861 --> 00:11:46,791
hold it in place.

154
00:11:46,792 --> 00:11:51,120
And so extensive that materials that had been earmarked for the construction of

155
00:11:51,121 --> 00:11:54,601
20,000 department units were used to build the wall.

156
00:11:54,602 --> 00:11:57,680
The makeshift primitive look of the structure,

157
00:11:58,540 --> 00:11:59,980
barbed wire visible everywhere,

158
00:12:00,660 --> 00:12:02,060
nakedly announced its intent

159
00:12:02,061 --> 00:12:05,060
to lock in anyone who might be thinking of leaving,

160
00:12:05,860 --> 00:12:07,240
including the men who built it.

161
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:09,960
I remember interviewing one of them

162
00:12:09,961 --> 00:12:12,320
and asked him how he actually felt about this,

163
00:12:12,460 --> 00:12:13,740
and he said that deep down,

164
00:12:14,380 --> 00:12:17,620
this really was gut-wrenching work,

165
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:19,540
that he really felt he was walling off

166
00:12:19,541 --> 00:12:22,660
part of his family from his other relatives.

167
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:25,200
On the other hand,

168
00:12:25,201 --> 00:12:29,200
Many in the East were relieved to see the government finally taking action.

169
00:12:29,201 --> 00:12:33,200
There was widespread resentment against the young professionals,

170
00:12:33,201 --> 00:12:37,200
trained at state expense, who had fled to the West.

171
00:12:37,201 --> 00:12:41,200
Moreover, World War II had exposed the horrors of German fascism,

172
00:12:41,201 --> 00:12:45,200
and there was a strong desire to take the country in a new direction.

173
00:12:45,201 --> 00:12:49,200
Many felt that the wall gave the socialist system a chance.

174
00:12:49,201 --> 00:12:54,200
The Berlin War had a special meaning,

175
00:12:54,201 --> 00:12:58,160
meaning, concerns, saving the socialist block.

176
00:12:58,161 --> 00:13:06,080
It was a necessity for us because there was an immense, rich world against us.

177
00:13:06,081 --> 00:13:09,360
And we had to overwhelm it.

178
00:13:09,361 --> 00:13:18,840
But stopping the flow of refugees with a wall would prove to be a difficult and bloody undertaking.

179
00:13:18,841 --> 00:13:23,480
Much of the barbed wire used in the first phase of the Berlin Wall was quietly imported

180
00:13:23,481 --> 00:13:25,362
from Poland and Czechoslovakia.

181
00:13:25,363 --> 00:13:28,640
The concrete posts were manufactured in East Germany's

182
00:13:28,641 --> 00:13:34,879
penal colonies. Historically, monumental walls have been few.

183
00:13:34,880 --> 00:13:37,640
Hadrian's Wall, marking the

184
00:13:38,360 --> 00:13:43,188
edge of the Roman Empire. The 1500-mile Great Wall of China.

185
00:13:43,189 --> 00:13:45,360
The Maginot Line, a wall of

186
00:13:45,361 --> 00:13:51,600
The Maginot Line, a wall of fortifications designed to keep the Germans out of France

187
00:13:51,601 --> 00:13:53,381
after the First World War.

188
00:13:53,382 --> 00:13:57,560
And the Atlantic Wall, put up by the Germans during World War

189
00:13:57,561 --> 00:14:01,856
II to block an invasion across the English Channel.

190
00:14:01,857 --> 00:14:04,720
But the Berlin Wall was different.

191
00:14:04,721 --> 00:14:08,583
Officially, it was called an anti-fascist protective barrier.

192
00:14:08,584 --> 00:14:10,040
But it was obvious from

193
00:14:10,041 --> 00:14:15,060
the start that its purpose was not to keep enemies out, but to keep the 1.1 million people

194
00:14:15,061 --> 00:14:22,240
of East Berlin in. Its impact on the city was immediate and profound. Following the

195
00:14:22,241 --> 00:14:27,600
post-World War II border between East and West Berlin, the wall blindly zigzagged through

196
00:14:27,601 --> 00:14:32,640
the city, cutting through streets, railroad tracks, bridges, and whatever else lay in

197
00:14:32,641 --> 00:14:33,341
its path.

198
00:14:33,342 --> 00:14:38,300
Fifteen public transportation routes that had tied East and West together were

199
00:14:38,301 --> 00:14:43,500
abruptly abandoned, creating eerie ghost stations through which no trains stopped.

200
00:14:45,100 --> 00:14:48,140
Innumerable families and friends were splintered and torn apart.

201
00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:54,780
You had these very, very tragic situations where a couple would get married in the West

202
00:14:54,781 --> 00:14:59,560
and would go to a predetermined point at the wall, sometimes the women wearing their wedding dresses,

203
00:14:59,900 --> 00:15:03,900
so that the relatives in the East could wave to them, but even that was forbidden.

204
00:15:03,901 --> 00:15:07,400
and they built the wall to such a height that people couldn't see over it

205
00:15:07,401 --> 00:15:11,400
and they bricked up the windows and it was an offence to wave to people in the west.

206
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:15,160
Some of the most harrowing dramas took place on Bernauer Street,

207
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:20,720
where a long row of apartment buildings stood on the exact border between East and West Berlin.

208
00:15:22,040 --> 00:15:26,180
People living in these houses, when they looked out of the window,

209
00:15:26,840 --> 00:15:31,960
their head was in the west side and their foot still in the east.

210
00:15:31,961 --> 00:15:37,600
So it was here on this side, it was very simple to escape in the first hours of the wall.

211
00:15:39,160 --> 00:15:44,460
Some desperate residents jumped into the nets of West Berlin firemen who encouraged them from the street below.

212
00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:48,280
At least two elderly people died when they missed the nets.

213
00:15:49,880 --> 00:15:53,880
The most dramatic incident involved 77-year-old Frieda Schulz,

214
00:15:54,520 --> 00:15:57,760
who was caught in a tug-of-war that epitomized the city's plight,

215
00:15:58,100 --> 00:16:01,320
with East German police trying to drag her back through the window.

216
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:03,920
and West Germans trying to pull her down.

217
00:16:07,780 --> 00:16:10,900
Authorities in the east moved quickly to seal off Bernauer Street,

218
00:16:11,540 --> 00:16:15,920
evicting 2,000 people from 87 buildings on September 20th,

219
00:16:16,220 --> 00:16:19,680
then bricking up 1,253 windows and doors.

220
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:24,180
The buildings were eventually demolished to make room for the wall.

221
00:16:25,880 --> 00:16:29,120
Bernauer Street wasn't the only weak link in the first-generation barrier.

222
00:16:29,121 --> 00:16:35,080
hundreds of people made it through poorly guarded sections of barbed wire which was used in place

223
00:16:35,081 --> 00:16:40,480
of stone in less populated parts of the city and on the rural border between East Germany and West

224
00:16:40,481 --> 00:16:49,160
Berlin others smashed vehicles including this homemade tank through thin barriers of concrete

225
00:16:49,161 --> 00:16:54,880
this section of the wall was knocked out by a truck in 1962

226
00:16:54,881 --> 00:17:00,526
too. Border guards were among the early escapees.

227
00:17:00,527 --> 00:17:05,480
85 guards fled to the west in the first six

228
00:17:05,481 --> 00:17:11,580
weeks of the wall, including one who was dramatically captured on camera as he tossed away his gun

229
00:17:11,581 --> 00:17:14,015
and jumped across a fence.

230
00:17:14,016 --> 00:17:19,540
Rudy Turo was a border guard from 1955, until he made a run

231
00:17:19,541 --> 00:17:23,994
for it with three civilians on February 21, 1962.

232
00:17:23,995 --> 00:17:27,720
Wearing his sergeant's uniform, he pulled

233
00:17:27,721 --> 00:17:31,857
rank on his fellow guards, ordering them to go to a different checkpoint.

234
00:17:31,858 --> 00:17:32,558
He then led

235
00:17:32,559 --> 00:17:33,871
the refugees to the wall.

236
00:17:33,872 --> 00:17:37,320
Things went well until one of them got tangled in barbed wire.

237
00:17:41,320 --> 00:17:47,080
At that moment, some other border guards who saw us immediately used their weapons,

238
00:17:47,081 --> 00:17:51,700
and I was forced to shoot continuously above their heads.

239
00:17:55,140 --> 00:17:58,580
And then we escaped into a house in West Berlin

240
00:17:58,581 --> 00:18:01,160
and we were freed by American soldiers.

241
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:05,640
Not all escapes were successful.

242
00:18:06,780 --> 00:18:09,280
The first person to be shot to death while crossing the wall

243
00:18:09,281 --> 00:18:12,540
was a 24-year-old tailor named Gunter Litwin.

244
00:18:13,380 --> 00:18:15,400
Litwin was killed on August 24th

245
00:18:15,401 --> 00:18:17,900
while trying to swim to the west across the Humboldt Canal.

246
00:18:21,420 --> 00:18:25,020
I went home on the 26th at three in the morning,

247
00:18:25,260 --> 00:18:28,260
where I found my mother crying in the apartment,

248
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:30,860
which had been completely taken apart.

249
00:18:31,960 --> 00:18:34,560
Nobody said that my brother had been shot.

250
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:36,380
We learned that in the evening,

251
00:18:36,580 --> 00:18:40,060
during the programme of the Berlin TV station SFB.

252
00:18:40,061 --> 00:18:44,680
I went to forensic medicine in order to find my brother,

253
00:18:44,681 --> 00:18:46,120
and identify him.

254
00:18:46,980 --> 00:18:48,140
I broke the coffin open

255
00:18:48,141 --> 00:18:51,920
and saw that my brother seemed to be completely unhurt.

256
00:18:52,480 --> 00:18:55,320
He had only a band-aid on the tip of his chin

257
00:18:55,321 --> 00:18:57,640
where the bullet had come out.

258
00:19:01,520 --> 00:19:04,480
Litvin's death sent a chilling message to everyone in Berlin.

259
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:07,780
But it was another death a few months later

260
00:19:07,781 --> 00:19:09,280
that shocked the city to its core.

261
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:13,440
18-year-old Peter Fechter was shot in the back

262
00:19:13,441 --> 00:19:15,840
while scaling the wall that used to stand right here,

263
00:19:16,260 --> 00:19:17,740
just in front of Checkpoint Charlie.

264
00:19:18,480 --> 00:19:21,940
For over an hour, he cried for help, but was left to die.

265
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:25,220
Both the American troops and the East German guards

266
00:19:25,221 --> 00:19:28,500
were afraid the other side might shoot if they tried to help.

267
00:19:29,420 --> 00:19:32,800
He was laying at the foot of this wall, crying out,

268
00:19:32,880 --> 00:19:33,860
help me, help me.

269
00:19:34,860 --> 00:19:37,080
And nobody could help him.

270
00:19:37,720 --> 00:19:39,500
They had to order three soldiers,

271
00:19:39,501 --> 00:19:42,000
and it took a long time to get the three soldiers there

272
00:19:42,001 --> 00:19:45,760
who went to retrieve him and carried him away like a rag doll.

273
00:19:46,720 --> 00:19:50,560
So this was at least a psychological signal

274
00:19:50,561 --> 00:19:55,960
for all those who had in their minds to cross the wall

275
00:19:55,961 --> 00:19:58,460
very risky.

276
00:19:59,460 --> 00:20:00,880
Don't do that.

277
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,400
If individual deaths were horrifying,

278
00:20:05,220 --> 00:20:07,800
the wall engendered other dramas that were nerve-wracking

279
00:20:07,801 --> 00:20:09,260
for the world.

280
00:20:10,200 --> 00:20:14,880
None more so than a showdown at Checkpoint Charlie between Soviet and American tanks

281
00:20:14,881 --> 00:20:21,300
that began on October 25, 1961, just two months after the wall was installed.

282
00:20:23,020 --> 00:20:26,175
Under the World War II Four Powers Agreement, the U.S.

283
00:20:26,176 --> 00:20:28,980
had absolute right of movement into East Berlin.

284
00:20:29,540 --> 00:20:35,620
But suddenly, East Berlin began to contest that right and would not let American jeeps pass without showing papers.

285
00:20:35,621 --> 00:20:42,180
In the jittery context of the Cold War, it was just the kind of incident that could spin out of control,

286
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:44,703
especially with 30 U.S.

287
00:20:44,704 --> 00:20:49,960
and 32 Soviet tanks facing each other, engines running, cannons loaded.

288
00:20:53,660 --> 00:20:55,611
The world was genuinely frightened.

289
00:20:55,612 --> 00:20:58,620
This was the Cuban Missile Crisis, but 40 yards apart.

290
00:20:58,920 --> 00:21:01,720
The worst case scenario was that somebody would lose a nerve.

291
00:21:01,721 --> 00:21:06,600
A soldier with a gun in a building would lose his nerve,

292
00:21:06,680 --> 00:21:09,000
and a tank would fire, and they're firing on us,

293
00:21:09,040 --> 00:21:11,280
we're firing on them, and it goes all the way back.

294
00:21:11,740 --> 00:21:13,840
The crisis ended after three days,

295
00:21:14,140 --> 00:21:17,140
when Khrushchev ordered the Soviet tanks to slowly back away,

296
00:21:17,440 --> 00:21:20,260
and the East Germans once again allowed U.S. soldiers

297
00:21:20,261 --> 00:21:22,060
to move freely in and out of Berlin.

298
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:26,920
Despite such showdowns, the wall continued to evolve.

299
00:21:26,921 --> 00:21:32,520
In 1963, the East Germans began working on a second-generation structure,

300
00:21:33,120 --> 00:21:37,280
made up of stacked concrete slabs that could hold up better against cars and trucks.

301
00:21:37,840 --> 00:21:41,620
And they methodically set about trying to plug any weak spots in the barrier.

302
00:21:42,880 --> 00:21:45,200
They really go over the whole wall.

303
00:21:45,680 --> 00:21:51,040
The leadership, Honneker and the generals, literally pace out the wall.

304
00:21:51,360 --> 00:21:53,480
They walk along every step of the way.

305
00:21:53,880 --> 00:21:56,360
They check out the sewers, they check out the rivers.

306
00:21:56,920 --> 00:22:01,420
And grills underwater are put in the rivers to stop swimmers getting through.

307
00:22:01,720 --> 00:22:05,420
Barriers are put in the underground sewers.

308
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:12,460
And by about 1963, the only real way to get across is by building tunnels.

309
00:22:13,360 --> 00:22:15,580
And later on, they got much more sophisticated.

310
00:22:16,220 --> 00:22:19,980
They had underground listening equipment where they could listen for tunnelling.

311
00:22:20,680 --> 00:22:24,940
They would build counter tunnels, which would actually collapse the other tunnels.

312
00:22:24,941 --> 00:22:35,740
By mid-1963, a solid wall made up of 7,874 cubic yards of concrete separated East and

313
00:22:35,741 --> 00:22:42,260
West Berlin. Barbed wire fencing, anchored by 116 wooden watchtowers, controlled the

314
00:22:42,261 --> 00:22:46,420
longer rural border between East Germany and West Berlin.

315
00:22:46,421 --> 00:22:52,438
A 100-yard border zone was added to the entire wall in June of 1963.

316
00:22:52,439 --> 00:22:54,120
Anyone entering the

317
00:22:54,121 --> 00:22:59,040
zone without proper authorization, was arrested.

318
00:22:59,041 --> 00:23:02,740
Although he had no intention of going to war over the wall,

319
00:23:02,741 --> 00:23:05,560
President Kennedy did buoy the spirits of West Berlin

320
00:23:05,561 --> 00:23:09,900
when he visited the city on June 26, 1963.

321
00:23:09,901 --> 00:23:14,400
1.5 million Berliners, nearly 70% of the population,

322
00:23:14,401 --> 00:23:16,460
turned out for his speech.

323
00:23:16,461 --> 00:23:20,180
All three men, wherever they may live,

324
00:23:20,181 --> 00:23:23,020
are citizens of Berlin.

325
00:23:23,021 --> 00:23:28,540
And therefore, as a free man, I take pride in the words,

326
00:23:29,540 --> 00:23:31,060
Ich bin ein Berliner.

327
00:23:32,540 --> 00:23:36,500
I think many West Berliners were extremely touched

328
00:23:36,501 --> 00:23:41,200
that Kennedy was prepared to come and show his solidarity,

329
00:23:41,760 --> 00:23:45,220
and that was, if you like, a political statement.

330
00:23:45,221 --> 00:23:50,160
It meant, I am prepared to defend democracy in the West.

331
00:23:50,161 --> 00:23:56,560
But Kennedy's visit also made it clear that the wall was a fact of life that was not about to disappear.

332
00:23:57,500 --> 00:24:02,800
Indeed, in purely physical terms, the Berlin Wall was only just beginning to take shape.

333
00:24:05,860 --> 00:24:09,840
At least 200 people were wounded trying to get through the Berlin Wall.

334
00:24:10,360 --> 00:24:13,420
Another 3,200 were arrested in the off-limits zone.

335
00:24:14,100 --> 00:24:17,580
An estimated 5,000 people successfully escaped.

336
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:28,000
From a design point of view, the bizarre challenge of the Berlin Wall was figuring out how to create a barrier

337
00:24:28,001 --> 00:24:32,820
that really could keep the 1.1 million people of East Berlin trapped inside.

338
00:24:34,340 --> 00:24:40,080
No single wall could do that, at least not one that the East Germans with their scarce resources could afford.

339
00:24:40,081 --> 00:24:47,440
instead they devised a wall system that used layer upon layer of surveillance

340
00:24:47,441 --> 00:24:57,540
detection obstruction and control by 1965 the wall was actually two walls each 10 to 12 feet

341
00:24:57,541 --> 00:25:04,640
high made of either concrete or wire mesh fence and separated by 30 to 100 yards depending on

342
00:25:04,641 --> 00:25:05,409
terrain.

343
00:25:05,410 --> 00:25:13,680
In between was the death strip, in which most would-be escapees were caught or killed.

344
00:25:15,820 --> 00:25:18,211
Here, the devil was truly in the details.

345
00:25:18,212 --> 00:25:21,360
Anyone who managed to scale the inner wall had to then

346
00:25:21,361 --> 00:25:26,880
get past a six-foot wire mesh fence, topped with rows of barbed wire and rigged with alarms.

347
00:25:27,960 --> 00:25:33,600
Beyond that lay landmines and self-activating firing mechanisms triggered by tripwire,

348
00:25:33,601 --> 00:25:35,500
which were deployed in rural areas.

349
00:25:35,920 --> 00:25:37,420
Those trying to escape by vehicle

350
00:25:37,421 --> 00:25:39,860
had to plow through upturned railroad tracks

351
00:25:39,861 --> 00:25:41,600
and an anti-tank ditch.

352
00:25:42,300 --> 00:25:43,860
Strategically spaced watchtowers,

353
00:25:44,580 --> 00:25:45,940
high-intensity searchlights,

354
00:25:46,620 --> 00:25:47,540
concrete bunkers,

355
00:25:48,220 --> 00:25:49,600
and armed patrols

356
00:25:50,440 --> 00:25:53,040
added to the morbid excess of urban lockdown.

357
00:25:54,920 --> 00:25:57,660
Both inner and outer wall were painted bright white,

358
00:25:58,320 --> 00:26:00,940
against which fugitives could be more easily detected.

359
00:26:00,941 --> 00:26:06,100
The land between the walls was chemically sprayed throughout the year.

360
00:26:07,300 --> 00:26:13,580
There was a space of about 50, maybe 100 meters, which was kept clear of any vegetation.

361
00:26:14,120 --> 00:26:23,340
Berlin is built on sand, so it was very sandy, and it would be raked clean every day to show any footprints that showed up.

362
00:26:24,580 --> 00:26:27,400
Dogs provided yet another layer of detection.

363
00:26:27,401 --> 00:26:33,080
The dogs were tethered on stakes on long leads so they could roam.

364
00:26:33,540 --> 00:26:36,720
The leashes extended from one dog to the next

365
00:26:36,721 --> 00:26:39,320
to the point where they were virtually nose to nose

366
00:26:39,321 --> 00:26:40,620
but couldn't attack each other,

367
00:26:41,140 --> 00:26:43,740
but were so close that nobody could run between them.

368
00:26:44,480 --> 00:26:47,080
Meanwhile, the wall itself continued to evolve.

369
00:26:47,740 --> 00:26:51,580
In 1968, the old improvised barrier was taken down

370
00:26:51,581 --> 00:26:53,940
and replaced with a third-generation wall,

371
00:26:53,941 --> 00:26:59,820
A quote, modern border, made up of prefabricated sections and anchored by steel beams.

372
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:03,440
Guards, of course, were the key to the whole system.

373
00:27:04,620 --> 00:27:08,900
8,000 border guards were carefully selected for their ideological reliability.

374
00:27:09,920 --> 00:27:14,120
Berliners were excluded, because it was thought they might balk at shooting other Berliners.

375
00:27:15,340 --> 00:27:17,960
Those with relatives in the West were also disqualified.

376
00:27:20,400 --> 00:27:23,040
Guarding the border was both tedious and nerve-wracking.

377
00:27:23,041 --> 00:27:27,920
with long shifts, harsh conditions, and disturbing orders.

378
00:27:32,240 --> 00:27:36,460
In the unit where I was deployed, the morale was devastated.

379
00:27:37,240 --> 00:27:42,360
Many soldiers had to serve for 12 hours, and the discipline was disastrous.

380
00:27:43,720 --> 00:27:49,180
They drank a lot of alcohol because a lot of comrades did not agree with the use of weapons,

381
00:27:49,880 --> 00:27:52,020
in other words, with the orders to fire.

382
00:27:53,040 --> 00:27:59,060
them sheathbafir. They were generally armed with kalashnikov semi-automatic

383
00:27:59,061 --> 00:28:06,060
rifles and very soon after the war went up there was a so-called shoot to kill

384
00:28:06,061 --> 00:28:10,820
order and it's quite clear from the internal orders which Honnecker

385
00:28:10,821 --> 00:28:17,880
personally supervised that guard order guards were expected to shoot as a last

386
00:28:17,881 --> 00:28:21,880
resort, but they were expected to shoot and not to miss.

387
00:28:21,881 --> 00:28:29,640
Indeed, East German President Walter Ulbricht himself presented this flag to the guards,

388
00:28:29,641 --> 00:28:34,480
who killed Peter Fechter, honoring them as the best border unit of 1962.

389
00:28:34,481 --> 00:28:43,880
Walter Ulbricht went to the border guards to say thank you, thank you for a dead one

390
00:28:43,881 --> 00:28:44,880
at the wall.

391
00:28:44,881 --> 00:28:49,740
He had a special flag produced with his own portrait on it.

392
00:28:50,880 --> 00:28:54,280
We will gain victory with Walter Ulbricht.

393
00:28:55,420 --> 00:28:59,320
When you look at it from the East German point of view,

394
00:28:59,840 --> 00:29:01,880
they had to motivate those people.

395
00:29:02,240 --> 00:29:05,560
Otherwise, they would flee themselves.

396
00:29:06,120 --> 00:29:10,580
And, well, at least several ones have done so.

397
00:29:10,581 --> 00:29:14,740
To reduce the risk of escape, guards were always paired up.

398
00:29:14,880 --> 00:29:16,680
Strangers controlling each other.

399
00:29:17,560 --> 00:29:20,020
Fully one-fifth of the guards belonged to the Stasi,

400
00:29:20,420 --> 00:29:22,480
East Germany's notorious secret police.

401
00:29:23,560 --> 00:29:28,340
So there were these strange feeling-out conversations

402
00:29:28,341 --> 00:29:31,940
between two of them who didn't quite trust each other

403
00:29:31,941 --> 00:29:34,240
and didn't know if the other one was secret service

404
00:29:34,241 --> 00:29:35,260
or would report on them.

405
00:29:35,340 --> 00:29:38,360
So you have two people not trusting the other one.

406
00:29:38,880 --> 00:29:44,680
Looking out across possibly 10 or 15 yards to a wall

407
00:29:44,681 --> 00:29:48,900
and then the west with this galaxy of neon.

408
00:29:50,160 --> 00:29:51,960
The watchtowers were spartan.

409
00:29:52,780 --> 00:29:55,440
No toilets, no cooking facilities, no heat.

410
00:29:56,680 --> 00:29:59,920
Trips to the bathroom and shift changeovers were carefully coordinated.

411
00:30:01,200 --> 00:30:04,660
To replace them, a jeep came along with two replacements.

412
00:30:06,060 --> 00:30:08,160
And one of the replacements went up to the watchtower

413
00:30:08,161 --> 00:30:10,260
and replaced one who went down,

414
00:30:10,540 --> 00:30:12,380
and the second one went up from the jeep

415
00:30:12,381 --> 00:30:14,160
and replaced the one who went down.

416
00:30:14,680 --> 00:30:18,380
and they drove off, so that at no stage were they off balance.

417
00:30:19,620 --> 00:30:23,800
Adding to the queasy strain of the job was the fact that it was dangerous.

418
00:30:24,840 --> 00:30:28,480
16 border guards were shot to death while serving at the Berlin Wall.

419
00:30:29,700 --> 00:30:33,600
And this became, if you like, part of the propaganda in East Germany

420
00:30:33,601 --> 00:30:38,260
that these were the guards of peace at the border between two worlds,

421
00:30:38,261 --> 00:30:41,720
and monuments would actually be set up to the guards.

422
00:30:41,721 --> 00:30:51,000
But after the end of the war, it was discovered that about half of these men were actually killed by their own troops in firefights,

423
00:30:51,160 --> 00:30:59,660
where one guard was trying to escape from his fellow and decided to make sure and put a bullet in his fellow guard.

424
00:31:00,660 --> 00:31:04,680
And that was something which East Berliners did not know at the time.

425
00:31:04,681 --> 00:31:11,240
Something else that no one knew, of course, was that the Berlin Wall, a seemingly permanent

426
00:31:11,241 --> 00:31:17,960
piece of the Cold War landscape, was living on borrowed time.

427
00:31:17,961 --> 00:31:22,920
After two families escaped over the wall by hot air balloon in 1979, the East Germans

428
00:31:22,921 --> 00:31:25,520
restricted the sale of nylon.

429
00:31:25,521 --> 00:31:29,480
Rope and diving equipment were also tightly controlled.

430
00:31:29,481 --> 00:31:36,480
By the 1970s, East Germany had evolved into one of the strongest economies in Eastern Europe.

431
00:31:36,481 --> 00:31:41,620
The turnaround was due in large part to the stabilizing influence of the wall,

432
00:31:41,621 --> 00:31:47,460
which effectively cut off the brain drain of refugees and kept the social system intact.

433
00:31:48,560 --> 00:31:53,480
But rising stature in the world community made East Germany increasingly sensitive

434
00:31:53,481 --> 00:31:56,440
about its most famous and embarrassing piece of infrastructure.

435
00:31:56,441 --> 00:32:04,880
In 1975, the East Germans began constructing a fourth-generation wall, a makeover designed

436
00:32:04,881 --> 00:32:08,840
to give the barrier a cleaner, more respectable look.

437
00:32:08,841 --> 00:32:12,660
It was also designed to make the wall even tougher to breach.

438
00:32:12,661 --> 00:32:17,500
Dozens of East Berliners were still trying to escape each year, usually at checkpoints

439
00:32:17,501 --> 00:32:21,240
with false papers, but sometimes through the wall.

440
00:32:21,241 --> 00:32:25,320
The latest renovation would make that more difficult.

441
00:32:25,321 --> 00:32:34,320
45,000 easy to install, weather and pollution resistant, L-shaped segments replaced the old wall that separated the two Berlins.

442
00:32:34,321 --> 00:32:41,320
The bottom of the L extended horizontally more than six feet in the ground to make the wall impregnable to cars and trucks.

443
00:32:41,321 --> 00:32:46,320
Each segment was 3.9 feet wide and 11.8 feet high.

444
00:32:46,321 --> 00:32:54,320
The top of the wall featured a new refinement, a rounded crown made of sewage piping that made it even harder to scale.

445
00:32:55,320 --> 00:33:05,640
It was very difficult to go over and some tried it from the west even as a provocation,

446
00:33:05,641 --> 00:33:11,060
but they fall down because it's very difficult to stay on the wall.

447
00:33:11,061 --> 00:33:13,200
In this sense it was perfected.

448
00:33:13,201 --> 00:33:16,040
And of course the material of the wall.

449
00:33:16,041 --> 00:33:20,160
When you look at it you can see it right now at the remains of the wall.

450
00:33:20,161 --> 00:33:26,560
There was steel in it and concrete which was very, very tight.

451
00:33:26,561 --> 00:33:32,900
So it would even resist, I think, a light tank.

452
00:33:32,901 --> 00:33:39,200
The impregnability of the fourth generation wall was tested by the border guards themselves.

453
00:33:39,201 --> 00:33:45,200
Mockups of the wall were built at their training barracks southeast of Berlin and initially

454
00:33:45,201 --> 00:33:47,560
they would try to defeat the wall.

455
00:33:47,561 --> 00:33:50,860
They would try to pole vault it, they would try to blow it up.

456
00:33:51,380 --> 00:33:55,760
And in one instance, they used an old T-34 tank to try and ram the wall.

457
00:33:56,460 --> 00:33:57,980
And they were always defeated.

458
00:33:58,880 --> 00:34:03,600
The cleaner-looking revised wall may have been bad news for people who wanted to get out.

459
00:34:04,060 --> 00:34:07,760
But it did have a silver lining, at least for some in the West.

460
00:34:08,260 --> 00:34:13,080
This is the wall which became a gift to Western graffiti artists.

461
00:34:13,081 --> 00:34:17,860
It was just so smooth and irresistible that they would cover it in graffiti.

462
00:34:18,580 --> 00:34:27,020
And at regular intervals, the East German border guards would go out through the little secret doors they had in the wall and respray it white.

463
00:34:27,300 --> 00:34:32,580
But within weeks, the thing would be a multicolored giant paint board, really.

464
00:34:35,160 --> 00:34:38,220
The image makeover continued into the 1980s.

465
00:34:38,221 --> 00:34:43,280
One by one, the more visually disturbing components of the wall system were eliminated.

466
00:34:44,940 --> 00:34:50,680
The tank barriers made of upright railroad tracks and the automatic firing mechanisms.

467
00:34:51,840 --> 00:34:55,240
But East Germany had no intention of doing away with the wall.

468
00:34:55,960 --> 00:35:03,100
Indeed, the government proudly celebrated the 25th birthday of its anti-fascist protective barrier in 1986.

469
00:35:03,101 --> 00:35:12,100
At the same time, it began drawing up plans for a minimally manned 21st century version of the structure, dubbed Border Wall 2000.

470
00:35:13,640 --> 00:35:23,140
Plans were being made to use microwaves, to use electronic technology, to use heat sensors to detect escapers.

471
00:35:23,920 --> 00:35:31,660
I think this would have cost the state an enormous amount of money, but given past experience, the East Germans would have spent that money.

472
00:35:31,661 --> 00:35:33,660
If you seek peace...

473
00:35:34,180 --> 00:35:36,720
As it turned out, they never got the chance.

474
00:35:37,380 --> 00:35:41,580
Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall.

475
00:35:42,900 --> 00:35:46,900
By the time President Reagan made this appeal in Berlin in 1987,

476
00:35:47,900 --> 00:35:50,400
forces were already in motion that would bring the wall

477
00:35:50,401 --> 00:35:54,300
and the division of the world that it represented crashing down.

478
00:35:56,060 --> 00:36:01,160
When Soviet President Mikhail Gorbachev announced a new policy of perestroika,

479
00:36:01,161 --> 00:36:07,080
or reform, he made it clear that the Soviet Union would not intervene militarily in Eastern Bloc

480
00:36:07,081 --> 00:36:07,781
affairs.

481
00:36:07,782 --> 00:36:14,520
Protestors throughout East Germany, and especially East Berlin, began demanding radical

482
00:36:14,521 --> 00:36:18,522
changes, including the right to free movement and travel.

483
00:36:18,523 --> 00:36:20,980
As demonstrations grew increasingly

484
00:36:20,981 --> 00:36:26,820
massive and defiant, the aging East German Politburo finally began to think about taking

485
00:36:26,821 --> 00:36:27,820
down the wall.

486
00:36:27,821 --> 00:36:34,820
It was the beginning of some realistic consideration of what is happening around us.

487
00:36:34,821 --> 00:36:43,400
But it was also too late to, by changing some accents of the communist policy, to gain the

488
00:36:43,401 --> 00:36:47,400
trust of the people.

489
00:36:47,401 --> 00:36:53,300
On November 9th, 1989, Gunter Schabowski made this stunning announcement.

490
00:36:53,301 --> 00:36:57,300
All travel restrictions between East and West Berlin were over.

491
00:36:57,301 --> 00:37:01,300
The wall had come down.

492
00:37:01,301 --> 00:37:04,300
Ironically, for a system in which so much was controlled,

493
00:37:04,301 --> 00:37:08,300
this momentous decision was announced without ever informing the border guards,

494
00:37:08,301 --> 00:37:12,300
who were immediately swamped with people trying to get to the West.

495
00:37:12,301 --> 00:37:15,300
It was a very dangerous misunderstanding.

496
00:37:15,301 --> 00:37:17,300
The soldiers did not know and trembled,

497
00:37:17,301 --> 00:37:19,300
what shall we do? And some will say,

498
00:37:19,301 --> 00:37:22,300
go back, that's a mob, and they will shoot.

499
00:37:22,301 --> 00:37:25,780
And the real wonder is that it did not happen.

500
00:37:26,980 --> 00:37:29,500
Fortunately, in the chaos of November 9th,

501
00:37:29,800 --> 00:37:32,940
no names were added to the list of those shot and killed

502
00:37:32,941 --> 00:37:34,940
trying to get through the Berlin Wall.

503
00:37:37,060 --> 00:37:40,620
In 1983, East Germany agreed to dismantle

504
00:37:40,621 --> 00:37:44,240
the 54,000 controversial automatic firing mechanisms

505
00:37:44,241 --> 00:37:46,040
that guarded the death strip

506
00:37:46,041 --> 00:37:50,120
in exchange for 2 billion Deutschmarks in loans from West Germany.

507
00:37:52,300 --> 00:38:03,300
After nearly three decades, hundreds of killings, thousands of escapes, and the occasional nuclear scare, the Berlin Wall had finally come down.

508
00:38:07,940 --> 00:38:12,580
For many who had grown up with the wall, it was hard to believe that it was really history.

509
00:38:14,040 --> 00:38:20,540
One way to make sure was by attacking the wall, physically, as thousands of Berliners gleefully did.

510
00:38:20,541 --> 00:38:29,280
It was not just something to take home, something which would be history maybe a year or two later.

511
00:38:29,980 --> 00:38:37,200
It was also, I think, some kind of accumulated frustration, accumulated anger

512
00:38:37,201 --> 00:38:43,940
that led a lot of West Berliners to make their own contribution to get the wall down.

513
00:38:43,941 --> 00:38:50,940
Official demolition of the 96-mile double wall began on June 13, 1990.

514
00:38:50,941 --> 00:38:56,940
It was a major undertaking, with 45,000 2.7-ton segments,

515
00:38:56,941 --> 00:39:03,940
127.5 kilometers of electrical fencing and 302 watchtowers.

516
00:39:03,941 --> 00:39:08,940
Ironically, it fell to the border guards to bring it down.

517
00:39:08,941 --> 00:39:13,940
It was constructed not to be destroyed, and now it had to be destroyed.

518
00:39:13,941 --> 00:39:23,940
So that was the last job of the border guards, that they had to take care of the remains of the wall.

519
00:39:23,941 --> 00:39:28,940
After they finished their job, there was nothing to do for them anymore.

520
00:39:28,941 --> 00:39:33,940
Most of the wall was crushed and recycled as pavement.

521
00:39:33,941 --> 00:39:44,740
Some 250 of the more brilliantly painted segments were carefully removed by the German state and sold at auction in Monte Carlo, generating over $1 million.

522
00:39:46,240 --> 00:39:55,740
Large chunks were sent to bastions of the West as trophies of the Cold War, the CIA, the Reagan Library.

523
00:39:59,040 --> 00:40:01,740
Other segments wound up in curious places.

524
00:40:01,741 --> 00:40:05,640
an outdoor cafe on 53rd Street in Midtown Manhattan

525
00:40:05,641 --> 00:40:13,460
and a urinal in the Main Street Station Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas.

526
00:40:16,340 --> 00:40:18,920
Components of the wall system were also sold off

527
00:40:18,921 --> 00:40:22,540
including the vicious guard dogs, hundreds of them

528
00:40:22,541 --> 00:40:26,380
which had been trained to turn on their own handlers if they tried to make a break.

529
00:40:26,381 --> 00:40:30,280
They turned out to be so docile that people bought them as pets.

530
00:40:31,740 --> 00:40:38,360
The killer dogs turned out to be wonderful, wonderful pets who didn't molest burglars or anything.

531
00:40:38,940 --> 00:40:42,260
The fall of the wall dramatically changed the look of Berlin.

532
00:40:43,960 --> 00:40:50,120
Incredibly, the death strip across the heart of the city was now choice real estate, and developers moved in.

533
00:40:51,620 --> 00:40:59,320
Potsdamer Platz, which in 1989 looked like this, is now a favourite corporate address, home to Daimler Benz and Sony.

534
00:40:59,321 --> 00:41:04,180
tourists once again roam around the Brandenburg Gate

535
00:41:04,181 --> 00:41:06,200
where for 28 years

536
00:41:06,201 --> 00:41:08,560
a stroll could have cost them their lives

537
00:41:08,561 --> 00:41:13,260
and in a mingling of grotesqueries

538
00:41:13,261 --> 00:41:15,980
the former death strip will become the new home

539
00:41:15,981 --> 00:41:18,260
for a World War II Holocaust memorial

540
00:41:18,261 --> 00:41:21,960
a vast monument designed to look like an off-kilter cemetery

541
00:41:21,961 --> 00:41:26,080
the only preserved section of the death strip

542
00:41:26,081 --> 00:41:27,400
is on Bernauer Street

543
00:41:27,401 --> 00:41:30,140
where a memorial has been set up

544
00:41:30,141 --> 00:41:33,300
with tiny slits in the East Berlin side of the wall

545
00:41:33,301 --> 00:41:36,380
that afford a glimpse of what was once no man's land.

546
00:41:40,180 --> 00:41:42,940
The wall itself is almost entirely gone

547
00:41:42,941 --> 00:41:44,900
except for the East Side Gallery

548
00:41:44,901 --> 00:41:48,160
and a few remnants that have been salvaged

549
00:41:48,161 --> 00:41:50,620
or that nobody bothered to tear down.

550
00:41:50,621 --> 00:41:57,440
A line of stone traces its former path,

551
00:41:58,040 --> 00:42:01,040
zigzagging through Berlin like footprints from the past.

552
00:42:03,280 --> 00:42:06,920
The past lingers less visibly in the ongoing attempt

553
00:42:06,921 --> 00:42:09,380
to sort through questions of guilt and responsibility

554
00:42:09,381 --> 00:42:11,840
for the killings that took place at the wall.

555
00:42:13,140 --> 00:42:14,560
Who was responsible?

556
00:42:14,960 --> 00:42:16,740
Is it the people who pulled the triggers,

557
00:42:17,120 --> 00:42:19,440
the ordinary guards at the wall?

558
00:42:19,441 --> 00:42:21,380
or was it their political bosses?

559
00:42:22,000 --> 00:42:24,740
At the end of the day, some of the political leaders

560
00:42:24,741 --> 00:42:27,200
did actually have to serve a prison sentence.

561
00:42:27,720 --> 00:42:29,940
In the cases of the trigger pullers,

562
00:42:30,580 --> 00:42:34,040
this was not something which the authorities could turn a blind eye to,

563
00:42:34,460 --> 00:42:36,040
so that trials were held,

564
00:42:36,220 --> 00:42:40,640
but generally the charge was one of manslaughter, not of murder.

565
00:42:41,140 --> 00:42:45,460
And in nearly all cases, a suspended sentence was given.

566
00:42:45,461 --> 00:42:53,420
East German President Ernst Honecker was put on trial in 1993, but released for ill health.

567
00:42:53,421 --> 00:42:57,340
He died in exile in Chile the next year.

568
00:42:57,341 --> 00:43:03,600
Gunter Schabowski, who served on the Palapuro between 1984 and 1989, when three people were

569
00:43:03,601 --> 00:43:06,860
killed at the wall, was sentenced to three years.

570
00:43:06,861 --> 00:43:10,860
He is the only high-ranking member of the East German government to take responsibility

571
00:43:10,861 --> 00:43:11,860
for the deaths.

572
00:43:11,861 --> 00:43:25,861
I was a member of the highest power and just to ignore the facts that people shall be killed

573
00:43:26,600 --> 00:43:34,100
if they wanted without any criminal intention just to leave the country.

574
00:43:35,100 --> 00:43:38,540
I'm also responsible for this.

575
00:43:38,541 --> 00:43:43,680
The concept of the wall is control.

576
00:43:44,580 --> 00:43:48,080
So if you control everything, you solve problems.

577
00:43:48,620 --> 00:43:53,160
This concept leads to death.

578
00:43:54,380 --> 00:43:56,980
And it took all your resources.

579
00:43:57,600 --> 00:44:01,600
So in a way, this wall building strikes back.

580
00:44:01,601 --> 00:44:11,620
And here you can learn from a country who was very perfect in doing it, it doesn't work.

581
00:44:12,480 --> 00:44:13,660
It doesn't work at all.

